- What disaster recovery steps for key compromise?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical cache invalidation design across multiple servers and cache backends, including stampedes, per-user invalidation, and pub/sub usage.

## Key Concepts
- Cache-aside pattern
- Per-user key naming and TTL
- Invalidation triggers for writes
- Distributed locks (SETNX/EX) to avoid stampede
- Pub/Sub advantages with Redis vs lack of notifications in Memcached

## Code Example
```javascript
async function getFeed(userId){
  const key = `feed:user:${userId}`
  const cached = await redis.get(key)
  if(cached) return JSON.parse(cached)
  const lock = `lock:feed:${userId}`
  if(await redis.set(lock, '1', 'NX', 'EX', 5)){
    const fresh = await computeFeed(userId)
    await redis.set(key, JSON.stringify(fresh), 'EX', 300)
    await redis.del(lock)
    return fresh
  } else {
    await sleep(50)
    return getFeed(userId)
  }
}

// Invalidation path (triggered by writes)
async function invalidateFeed(userId){
  const key = `feed:user:${userId}`
  await redis.del(key)
  if(usePubSub){
    await redis.publish(`feed-inval:${userId}`, 'invalidate')
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design distributed workflow with real systems concepts: saga orchestration, event sourcing, outbox pattern, and idempotent handlers under failure. Focuses on practical correctness and maintainability at scale.

## Key Concepts
- Saga orchestration across multiple services
- Event sourcing and outbox integration
- Idempotent handlers using correlationId and event.id
- Compensating actions for partial failures

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo orchestrator sketch
function onEvent(evt) {
  const key = `${evt.correlationId}:${evt.id}`;
  if (dedupe(key)) return;
  markHandled(key);
  switch (evt.type) {
    case 'OrderPlaced':
      enqueue('ReserveInventory', { orderId: evt.orderId, items: evt.items }, evt.correlationId);
      break;
    case 'InventoryReserved':
      enqueue('AuthorizePayment', { orderId: evt.orderId, amount: evt.total }, evt.correlationId);
      break;
    case 'PaymentAuthorized':
      enqueue('ShipOrder', { orderId: evt.orderId }, evt.correlationId);
      break;
    case 'OrderFailed':
      enqueue('ReleaseInventory', { orderId: evt.orderId }, evt.correlationId);
      enqueue('RefundPayment', { orderId: evt.orderId }, evt.correlationId);
      break;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a policy-driven, multi-source token issuance flow with per-tenant constraints and real-time evaluation.

## Key Concepts

- Lightweight policy decision point (PDP) for token issuance
- Per-tenant audience/issuer metadata and key rotation
- Context-based access controls: IP allowlist, geolocation, time window, device fingerprint
- Caching decisions to reduce latency and load

## Code Example

```javascript
// issuance after policy approval (sketch)
function issueJWTForPolicyApproved(payload, tenant, kid, ttlSec) {
  const now = Math.floor(Date.now()/1000);
  const exp = now + ttlSec;
  const tokenPayload = {
    iss: `https://idp.example/${tenant}`,
    aud: `https://api.${tenant}.service`,
    sub: payload.sub,
    iat: now,
    exp,
    nonce: payload.nonce,
    tenant
  };
  return signJWT(tokenPayload, kid);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test policy changes without affecting production tokens?
- How would you model multi-tenant policy conflicts and roll back safely?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical OAuth2 debugging skills, understanding of token lifecycle, and real-world troubleshooting abilities that backend engineers face daily.

## Expected Answer
Strong candidates identify: 1) Refresh token expired/revoked, 2) Scope mismatch between access and refresh tokens, 3) Client configuration issues. They should mention checking token introspection endpoint, reviewing logs, and testing with Postman/curl.

## Code Example
```typescript
// Debug refresh token failure
async function debugRefreshToken(refreshToken: string) {
  try {
    // 1. Check token introspection
    const introspect = await fetch('/oauth/introspect', {
      method: 'POST',
      body: `token=${refreshToken}`
    });

    // 2. Validate client configuration
    const clientConfig = await getClientConfig();

    // 3. Review token storage
    const storedToken = await getStoredToken(refreshToken);

    return { introspect, clientConfig, storedToken };
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Debug failed:', error);
  }
}
```

