## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical understanding of saga orchestration, CQRS, and event-sourcing in a tangible, beginner-friendly scenario with compensations.

## Key Concepts
- Saga orchestration across Inventory, Payments, Shipping
- Compensating actions and failure handling
- Event contracts, idempotency, Outbox pattern
- EventStore and read-model updates

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal event-contracts and command routing
type EventEnvelope = { eventId: string; correlationId: string; type: string; payload: any };
type Command = { type: string; payload: any };
const mapEventToCommands = (e: EventEnvelope): Command[] => {
  switch (e.type) {
    case 'PlaceOrder': return [{ type: 'InventoryReserve', payload: { orderId: e.payload.orderId } }];
    case 'InventoryReserved': return [{ type: 'ChargePayment', payload: { orderId: e.payload.orderId } }];
    case 'PaymentCharged': return [{ type: 'ShipOrder', payload: { orderId: e.payload.orderId } }];
    default: return [];
  }
};
```

## Why This Is Asked
Real-world setups use multiple IdPs; token replay and cross-provider binding are critical issues.

## Key Concepts
- JWKS caching and key rotation per provider
- Replay protection with jti in distributed cache
- Token binding via CNF and mTLS
- Revocation propagation and token lifecycle

## Code Example
```javascript
function verifyJwt(token, provider) {
  const key = getJwksKey(provider, token.kid);
  const payload = jwtVerify(token, key);
  if (Date.now() >= payload.exp * 1000) throw new Error('expired');
  if (redis.exists(`jti:${payload.jti}`)) throw new Error('replay');
  redis.set(`jti:${payload.jti}`, 1, 'EX', payload.exp - now);
  return payload;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle JWKS rotation events?
- How would you monitor and debug cross-provider revocation flows?
